/* Main computational kernel. The whole function will be timed,
   including the call and return.

   Optimized version notes
   -----------------------

   The original kernel implemented the BLAS SYMM operation using a
   loop nest of the form

       for i
         for j
           temp2 = 0
           for k<i
             C[k][j] += alpha * B[i][j] * A[i][k];
             temp2   += B[k][j] * A[i][k];
           C[i][j] = beta*C[i][j] + alpha*B[i][j]*A[i][i] + alpha*temp2;

   which is a direct transcription of the Fortran reference BLAS where
   arrays are column-major.  In C, arrays are row-major, so the access
   pattern C[k][j] / B[k][j] (k varying in the innermost loop) has a
   large stride and poor cache locality.

   The optimized kernel below:

     * Restructures the computation algebraically so that:
         - j (the column index) is always the innermost loop.
         - B and C are accessed along rows (contiguous in memory).
     * Pre-scales C by beta in a separate pass:
         C := beta * C,
       and then computes
         C := C + alpha * A * B,
       using the lower triangle of A, exactly matching the original
       numerical result (up to floating-point round-off).
     * Avoids any temporary 2D buffers; only row pointers are used.

   The transformation is mathematically equivalent to the original
   kernel: for each (i,j), the final value of C[i][j] is

       C[i][j] = beta*C0[i][j]
                 + alpha * A[i][i] * B[i][j]
                 + alpha * sum_{k<i} A[i][k] * B[k][j]
                 + alpha * sum_{k>i} A[k][i] * B[k][j],

   which is exactly what the original code computes.  We simply
   accumulate the symmetric off-diagonal contributions in a different,
   cache-friendly order.
